refuted_summary.defect:
The 'in context' token counts in Table 7 — and therefore the token-matched subsets that define Tables 8 and 9 — are measured on a surrogate string that no measured call ever used. probe_name_length.py tokenizes `f"Candidate: {name}
"`, but every row in the names study was produced from `f"Job posting:
{POSTING}

Candidate:
Name: {name}
{body}

{ask}"`. The left context differs ('Name: ' vs 'Candidate: ') and the right context differs (résumé body vs end of string). The paper's argument is that 'the tokens are what the model receives' and the paper elsewhere boasts that 'Every row records the SHA-256 of the exact prompt that produced it, so a claim that two cells received identical input is verifiable after the fact rather than assumed' — but this claim is not verified against the actual prompts. The artifact itself shows context is not neutral: on Llama-3.1 mean delta is +0.75 isolated and +0.92 in the probe's context, so a third context could give a third answer and a different membership for the 12-pair subset.

refuted_summary.why_refuted:
The auditor got the string comparison right and the consequence wrong. It is true that probe_name_length.py (paper-a/src/probe_name_length.py:73-74) tokenizes `f"Candidate: {name}
"` while the names study builds `f"Job posting:
{POSTING}

Candidate:
Name: {name}
{body}

{ask}"` (experiment_delta_stability.py:186-190, imported by experiment_names.py:57). But the auditor never tested whether that difference changes anything, and inferred it must because mean_delta_isolated (0.75) differs from mean_delta_in_context (0.9167) on Llama-3.1. That comparison does not support the inference: isolated-vs-context differs for a known reason — in isolation the name starts the string with no leading space, so "Aisha" tokenizes differently from " Aisha". It says nothing about whether two contexts that BOTH place the name after ": " and before "
" differ. They are the same local context, which is exactly what the probe's surrogate preserves.

I reproduced the tokenizers from scratch and checked it. I wrote a stdlib GGUF metadata reader plus pure-Python reimplementations of llama.cpp's BPE (llama-bpe pretokenizer regex + merge ranks) and SPM (score-priority bigram merge with byte fallback) tokenizers, reading the vocab straight out of the same .gguf files the probe served. Validation first: my implementations reproduce ALL 384 recorded token counts (48 pairs x 2 arms x {isolated, in-context}) in name_length_llama-3.1-8b-instruct.json, name_length_llama-2-7b-chat.json, name_length_mistral-7b-instruct-v0.1.json and name_length_mistral-7b-instruct-v0.3.json with zero mismatches. So the reimplementation is the served tokenizer on these strings.

Then the actual test: for every one of the 48 pairs, every one of the 12 wording variants and both templates the names study ran (TEMPLATES_2 = T1_strong, T3_marginal), I built the real `user_message(variant, name, body)` for both arms and took the difference in full-prompt token counts — 1,152 real prompts per arm per model, 9,216 tokenizations per model, 36,864 total. Result on all four models: the real-prompt delta equals the probe's delta_in_context for every pair (0 disagreements out of 48 per model), and it is constant across all 24 variant x template cells for every pair (0 pairs with a variant-dependent delta). Mean delta: Llama-3.1 +0.9167 real vs +0.9167 probe; Llama-2 +0.2500 vs +0.2500; Mistral v0.1 +0.5000 vs +0.5000; Mistral v0.3 +0.5000 vs +0.5000 — matching Table 7's +0.92/+0.25/+0.50/+0.50 exactly. Token-matched subset membership (the set of pairs with delta==0, which analyze_name_length.py:98,137 uses to define Tables 8 and 9) is set-identical between probe context and real prompt on all four models, with n = 12/15/16/16 — exactly the n column in Tables 8 and 9.

The mechanism is unsurprising once tested. In both contexts the name sits between ": " and "
". Llama-3's pretokenizer splits on that boundary before BPE runs, and llama-2/Mistral's SPM has no merge crossing the byte-fallback newline, so the resume body downstream cannot reach back across it. The surrogate is not arbitrary — it preserves the exact left and right delimiters of the real prompt, which is the property that matters.

So the alleged defect does not exist: Tables 7, 8 and 9 are numerically unchanged, subset membership is unchanged, and the Table 9 Llama-3.1 result (-0.1509 -> -0.2423, p=0.004) stands. The auditor's own proposed fix says "if the deltas are unchanged, say so" — they are unchanged. The one residual is cosmetic: the caption's "in context" is under-specified and could name the delimiters, but that is wording polish, not a major defect, and no number in the paper moves.
